1) Is it realistic?
     No; drowning in a wading pool is NOT realistic, unless one is
confused, stunned, or hallucinating.  People do not commonly jump into
pools while laden with plate mail.  On top of that, most of us humans
can, if not swim, at least bob when not wearing heavy armor; by all
means they aren't going to drop into a pool of water and drown.

2) Is it playable?
     Dave says it is.  He claims the worth of a pool is to "keep the
game threatening," or, if I understand, that they add to the quality of
a game by keeping one on one's toes.  I don't consider this to be a
useful quality.  I can live with sudden death *if it's realistic*.  For
instance, if pools were to be replaced with "bottomless pits" I might be
willing to live with it (although most likely they would not appear on
the shallower levels, and would not be created by fountain overflow). 
Perhaps on shallower levels, pools should at most be intrinsically
visible rust traps, and on lower levels bottomless pits could be
sprinkled in.  This, of course, doesn't fit in well with a moat around a
castle; maybe there should simply be moat monsters which do lots of
damage so that it's unfeasable to circumvent the castle (and if the
drawbridge is up, you can't get in that way either).  This would only be
in the castle moat, of course.  A moat monster would have trouble living
inside a wading pool created by an overzealous fountain.
     I also question the very idea of sudden death, however.  I think
there are two kinds: "devious" and "unlucky".  A "devious" sudden death
would be comparable to choking on one's food, reading a scroll of
genocide while confused (or genociding minotaurs while not realizing
that it's a cursed scroll), etc.; these add to the game by creating
STRATEGY problems.  "Unlucky" sudden deaths are stepping in pools of
water, walking down a staircase and being killed in one round by
Yeenoghu (can't happen, I don't think, but you get the idea), etc.;
these, at their very best, add nothing to the game but a SKILL problem. 
Nethack, instead of being a game of devising a survival strategy,
becomes a game of not making unrealistic mistakes of a non-strategic
nature.  While I've nothing against games of this type, they don't go
well with variable-speed (due to mtasking) Unix workstations and
overlayed IBM overlay code, and I don't consider Nethack as a game that
appeals much to that audience to start with ("Wow!  What a video game! 
I've been having *so much fun* dodging these tricky fountains... maybe
when I get up to clvl 11 they'll start to move!").
